arXiv:2511.23347v1 [cs.LG] 28 Nov 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bowen Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Matteo Zecchin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Osvaldo Simeone
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text">An earlier version of this paper was submitted in part at the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 4-8 May, Barcelona, Spain, 2026 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text">This work was partially supported by the Open Fellowships of the EPSRC (EP/W024101/1) and by the EPSRC project (EP/X011852/1).</span></span></span></span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text">The authors are with the King’s Communications, Learning and Information Processing (KCLIP) Lab, Centre for Intelligent Information Processing Systems, Department of Engineering, King’s College London, London WC2R 2LS, U.K. (e-mail: {bowen.wang, matteo.1.zecchin, osvaldo.simeone}@kcl.ac.uk).</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">An associative memory (AM) enables cue–response recall, and it has recently been recognized as a key mechanism underlying modern neural architectures such as Transformers. In this work, we introduce the concept of distributed dynamic associative memory (DDAM), which extends classical AM to settings with multiple agents and time-varying data streams. In DDAM, each agent maintains a local AM that must not only store its own associations but also selectively memorize information from other agents based on a specified interest matrix. To address this problem, we propose a novel tree-based distributed online gradient descent algorithm, termed DDAM-TOGD, which enables each agent to update its memory on the fly via inter-agent communication over designated routing trees. We derive rigorous performance guarantees for DDAM-TOGD, proving sublinear static regret in stationary environments and a path-length dependent dynamic regret bound in non-stationary environments. These theoretical results provide insights into how communication delays and network structure impact performance. Building on the regret analysis, we further introduce a combinatorial tree design strategy that optimizes the routing trees to minimize communication delays, thereby improving regret bounds. Numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed DDAM-TOGD framework achieves superior accuracy and robustness compared to representative online learning baselines such as consensus-based distributed optimization, confirming the benefits of the proposed approach in dynamic, distributed environments.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6><span id="id4" class="ltx_text">
Associative memory, distributed optimization, online convex optimization, regret analysis.
</span>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Context and Motivation</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An associative memory (AM), originally rooted in psychology and cognitive science, refers to the ability to store cue–response pairs and to recall the corresponding response when a cue is presented <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. For instance, seeing an image of a banana can evoke its distinctive smell and taste; hearing a person’s name can bring to mind their appearance, habits, or personality. This principle of cue-based recall has inspired developments across neuroscience, signal processing, and machine learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most recently, modern sequence models, notably the Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, have been interpreted as implementing an AM mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The attention operation in Transformers performs content-based retrieval of information, effectively recalling relevant <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">responses</span> (<span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">values</span>) based on presented <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cues</span> (<span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">keys</span>). In fact, inference in such models can be viewed as a form of online memory building and retrieval, which can be formalized via <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">online convex optimization</span> (OCO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, existing AM mechanisms are <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">centralized</span>: all information is processed and stored by a single model or agent.
In contrast, many real-world applications operate in inherently <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">distributed</span> environments.
As illustrated in Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, examples include sensor networks, internet-of-things (IoT) systems, and cellular wireless networks, where multiple agents, such as devices, sensors, or infrastructure nodes, observe only local data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
In such networks, an agent could benefit from recalling associations not only from its own data but also from other agents’ data relevant to its task.
For instance, in an IoT-based surveillance scenario with multiple camera sensors, each camera may capture a different viewpoint of the same scene or object.
By sharing information across cameras, the AM at one camera could recall information about the same object from multiple perspectives, improving tasks such as recognition and tracking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-agent networks, such as the aforementioned communication networks, data often arrive as a continuous, non-stationary stream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
Over time, the underlying data distribution and target associations may evolve.
This scenario is common in tasks including traffic analysis, in which data statistics vary as a function of the time of day and/or of the season <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
A static AM may become outdated as new data arrive or as the environment changes.
Therefore, it is important to develop a dynamic associative memory that can adapt online to non-stationary data, updating stored associations, retrieving new relevant information, and forgetting outdated associations as needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, many practical settings demand an online, distributed AM that enables multiple agents to jointly and adaptively memorize relevant information over time.
Motivated by these considerations, this paper focuses on the study of <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributed dynamic associative memory</em> (DDAM).
Our goal is to bridge the gap between classical centralized AM mechanisms and the requirements of distributed, time-varying environments.
In the following, we outline the state of the art and then summarize our contributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">State of the Art</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Associative Memory (AM):</span>
Early studies of AM trace back to the Hopfield network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, where memory retrieval is modeled as the minimization of an energy function toward attractor states representing stored patterns.
Building upon this formulation, subsequent works proposed dense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and continuous <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> Hopfield networks, which generalize the energy function and improve the memory capacity.
These models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> constitute a family of energy-based AMs, where inference corresponds to energy minimization.
More recently, Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and related sequence models have been interpreted as AM mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, where the attention operation performs content-based retrieval from representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
From this viewpoint, inference in such models can be seen as solving an online optimization problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for the storage and retrieval of information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online Convex Optimization (OCO):</span> OCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> is performed over a sequence of consecutive rounds, where at each round a learner selects a decision.
After the decision is made, a cost function is revealed and the learner incurs a corresponding loss.
The learner then updates its parameters based on the observed loss to improve subsequent performance.
This procedure may model AM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, where cues evoke responses that are iteratively corrected through feedback.
In OCO, the performance metric is the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regret</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which can be categorized as either static or dynamic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static regret</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> measures the difference between the cumulative loss incurred by the algorithm and that of the best fixed decision in hindsight.
In contrast, the <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dynamic regret</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> quantifies the performance gap with respect to a time-varying comparator sequence, capturing the algorithm’s adaptability to non-stationary environments.
To minimize regret, two of the most widely used algorithms are <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">online gradient descent</span> (OGD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">follow-the-regularized-leader</span> (FTRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Compared with FTRL, OGD is computationally simpler and faster, as it updates the model using only the current gradient without storing past information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distributed learning:</span> Distributed learning has been an active research direction for decades.
Depending on the learning objective, it can be broadly categorized into single-task learning and multi-task learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Within single-task distributed learning, it can be further divided into parameter-server–based and fully decentralized paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
In the parameter-server–based settings, the most well-known paradigm is federated learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, where each agent optimizes a local objective using its own data and periodically aggregates model updates to obtain a global model.
In contrast, fully decentralized learning is based on inter-agent message passing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, in consensus-based distributed learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, agents exchange information only with their neighbors and iteratively update to reach an agreement on a common model.
In multi-task distributed learning formulations, the problem is to learn multiple heterogeneous, but generally related tasks distributed over agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Existing approaches either infer task correlations from data-driven similarity measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> or define them explicitly through spatial or geographical adjacency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">I-C</span> </span><span id="S1.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce the problem of DDAM and propose a novel DDAM protocol with both static and dynamic regret guarantees.
The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formulation of DDAM:</span> As illustrated in Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, we formalize the DDAM problem, in which <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents collaboratively optimize their local associative memories in an online fashion.
Each agent <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> processes its own stream of data, and aims to memorize not only its local input–output associations but also information from a selected subset of other agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novel protocol – DDAM-TOGD:</span> We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DDAM via tree-based OGD</span> (DDAM-TOGD) as an efficient solution to the DDAM problem.
In DDAM-TOGD, each agent runs an instance of OGD that is augmented with network communication.
Specifically, every agent periodically broadcasts its memory parameters along a spanning tree that connects to all agents whose data it needs.
Those agents compute gradient feedback using their local data and send the gradients back along the reverse path.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regret analysis:</span> We rigorously analyze the performance of DDAM-TOGD in terms of regret.
Specifically, we prove that in static environments, DDAM-TOGD achieves sublinear static regret.
Hence, each agent’s memory asymptotically approaches the best <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fixed memory</span> in hindsight.
Moreover, for dynamic environments, we prove a dynamic regret bound that scales with the path-length of the time-varying optimal memories.
Accordingly, if the underlying optimal association for each agent changes slowly over time (small path-length), DDAM-TOGD can track these changes closely and incur low regret.
In contrast, if the environment changes more rapidly, the regret grows accordingly, which is inevitable in the worst case.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Communication-efficient tree design:</span> The regret analysis of DDAM-TOGD provides insights into how communication delays affect performance. Building on this insight, we propose a combinatorial optimization approach to design the communication trees for each agent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental validation:</span> We validate the proposed DDAM framework through extensive numerical experiments. We compare against existing online optimization baselines such as independent (non-cooperative) online learners and consensus-based distributed OGD. DDAM-TOGD is demonstrated to achieve lower regret and error rates across various scenarios, including both stationary and dynamic settings.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">I-D</span> </span><span id="S1.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Organization</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Sec. <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> defines the problem formulation.
Sec. <a href="#S3" title="III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> presents the state-of-the-art protocols.
Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Tree-based Online Gradient Descent ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> proposes the DDAM protocol and a communication-efficient tree design.
Sec. <a href="#S5" title="V Theoretical Analysis ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> provides a proof sketch of the regret guarantees.
Finally, Sec. <a href="#S6" title="VI Experiments ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> illustrates the experimental setting and results, and Sec. <a href="#S7" title="VII Conclusion ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Statement</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study DDAM in a setting with networked <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents.
As shown in Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, each agent <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> processes a stream of <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">keys</span> <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{n,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{n,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">values</span> <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{n,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{n,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which evolve over discrete time <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,...,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,...,T</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, the key <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> may consist of context samples in a time series, and the value <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> may include future samples of the same time series.
Alternatively, key <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and value <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> may represent embeddings of streaming tokens for multi-modal memory recall tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
The goal is to dynamically optimize an AM mechanism at each <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th agent, ensuring that at each time <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> not only recalls its past data <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{k}_{n,t^{\prime}},\mathbf{v}_{n,t^{\prime}}\}_{t^{\prime}=1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{k}_{n,t^{\prime}},\mathbf{v}_{n,t^{\prime}}\}_{t^{\prime}=1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also data <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{k}_{m,t^{\prime}},\mathbf{v}_{m,t^{\prime}}\}_{t^{\prime}=1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{k}_{m,t^{\prime}},\mathbf{v}_{m,t^{\prime}}\}_{t^{\prime}=1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that is processed by a subset of other agents <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\neq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\neq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setting</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Denote as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> the parameters of the AM mechanism maintained at agent <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the design domain for parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, for a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear AM</span>, the parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\times d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\times d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, and the AM mechanism returns the estimated value <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\mathbf{k}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\mathbf{k}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the input key <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, a linear AM mechanism may operate in a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space via a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\times d_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\times d_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> returning the output vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\phi(\mathbf{k}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\phi(\mathbf{k}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-linear feature-extraction function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The AM mechanism at agent <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> can be generally queried to recall data <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math> of any agent <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, including itself.
The performance of AM <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> at agent <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in recalling the data <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math> of agent <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is measured by a cost function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Commonly used memory retrieval cost functions for linear AM are of the form</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t})=\ell(\mathbf{X}_{m,t},\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})+\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t})=\ell(\mathbf{X}_{m,t},\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})+\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\mathbf{X}_{m,t},\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\mathbf{X}_{m,t},\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math> is a cost function comparing the true value <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{m,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{m,t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the value estimated based on memory <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{m,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{m,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math> is a regularization function.
Examples of cost functions for linear AM are summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Memory retrieval cost functions <math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> of the form (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) for different variants of Linear Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the memory matrix, <math id="S2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the key vector, and <math id="S2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding value vector. (<math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> is a gating vector with binary entries that is independent of <math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a feature-extraction function.)</figcaption>
<table id="S2.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S2.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Model</th>
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Linear attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\langle\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗𝐤</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\langle\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Gated linear attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\langle\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗𝐤</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\langle\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k},\mathbf{v}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\|\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1-\bm{\psi}})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝍</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\|\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1-\bm{\psi}})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">DeltaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{v}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗𝐤</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{X}\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{v}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Softmax attention w/o norm</th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Softmax attention w/ norm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;">Gated softmax attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\langle\mathbf{X}\phi(\mathbf{k}),\mathbf{v}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\|\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1-\bm{\psi}})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝍</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\|\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{1-\bm{\psi}})\mathbf{X}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall retrieval cost for agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> weights the cost <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the information from agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> through the parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq w_{n,m}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq w_{n,m}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m=1}^{N}w_{n,m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m=1}^{N}w_{n,m}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accordingly, the weight <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> dictates the relevance of the data streamed at agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> itself.
Mathematically, the cumulative cost function at time <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}=\{\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}=\{\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}=\{m\in\mathcal{N}|w_{n,m}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}=\{m\in\mathcal{N}|w_{n,m}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of agents <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> whose data is relevant to agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., those with nonzero weights <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The logical weights defining the cumulative cost (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) are collected into the row stochastic matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{W}]_{n,m}=w_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{W}]_{n,m}=w_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is assumed to be known at all agents (see Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>(b)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since the data <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math> are only available to agent <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, the cost function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m,t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m,t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> can only be evaluated by agent <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, in order to optimize the cost function (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), as seen in Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>(a), agent <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> must generally communicate with other agents, unless the agent <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> only recalls local data, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n,n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n,n}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
To enable inter-agent communication, agents interact over an <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">undirected graph</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{N},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=\{1,2,\cdots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=\{1,2,\cdots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of agent indices, and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a collection of inter-agent links <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that agents <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> can communicate.
We let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{n}=\{m\in\mathcal{N}:(m,n)\in\mathcal{E}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{n}=\{m\in\mathcal{N}:(m,n)\in\mathcal{E}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of neighbors of agent <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the set of edges <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the physical connectivity of the agents (see Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>(a)), while the weight matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the logical relationships among the performance requirements of different agents (see Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>(b)).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Definition</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we adopt a worst-case design approach, where optimality is evaluated with respect to the optimal solutions corresponding to the given sequences of keys and values observed by the agents.
Specifically, we investigate the following design objectives:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static regret:</span> The static regret is defined as the difference between the cumulative loss incurred by an online algorithm and the cumulative loss of the best fixed solutions <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> in hindsight.
The optimal solution for each agent <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{U}\in\mathcal{X}}\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{U}\in\mathcal{X}}\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which is generally different across agents.
Summing over the <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents, the static regret is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}\right)-\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}\right)-\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The static regret is relevant in stationary environments, for which there exists a single AM mechanism that can memorize well enough all data streamed within the time horizon <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic regret:</span> The static regret is not an appropriate design criterion for changing environments, in which no single AM mechanism can retrieve data with sufficient accuracy over the entire time horizon of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> time steps.
In this case, the static regret in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 1st item ‣ II-B Problem Definition ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) may be negative, since there may exist online AM policies that outperform the best static solution in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 1st item ‣ II-B Problem Definition ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), even if the latter has access in hindsight to the entire data to be memorized.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we also study the dynamic regret <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, in which the regret is evaluated against any sequence of changing AM mechanisms <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n}^{T}=\{\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n}^{T}=\{\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, as</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}\right)-\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}_{n}^{T}\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_{n}^{T}\right)-\mathcal{L}_{n}^{T}\left(\mathbf{U}_{n}^{T}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that static regret (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 1st item ‣ II-B Problem Definition ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) can be viewed as a special form of dynamic regret (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2nd item ‣ II-B Problem Definition ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) by setting the reference AM mechanism <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the fixed best solution in hindsight, i.e., <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n,t}=\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n,t}=\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all time step <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Technical Assumptions</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis in this paper assumes the following standard conditions, which have also been adopted in, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The design domain <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed, convex, and it includes the origin, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, the cost function <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math> is convex within the domain <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> at every time step <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that all cost functions listed in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> satisfy this assumption with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> being the space of all matrices of suitable dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The diameter of the design domain <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{Y}\|\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{Y}\|\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it follows that the norm of the AM parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded as <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{X}\|^{2}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{X}\|^{2}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every time step <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient of the cost function <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{n,t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{n,t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded in Euclidean norm by a constant <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;G_{n}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;G_{n}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\nabla f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X})\right\|\leq G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\nabla f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X})\right\|\leq G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conventional Protocols</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review conventional solutions that can be leveraged to design DDAM protocols.
Specifically, we first study the ideal case in which every agent has access to full information, i.e., to all functions <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{n,t}(\cdot)\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{n,t}(\cdot)\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we consider the special case of the DDAM problem introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, in which agents assign the same weight to the data of all agents, i.e., <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>𝟙𝟙</mn><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this setting, one can impose without loss of optimality that all agents share identical AM parameters, so that distributed consensus protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> provide suitable solutions to the DDAM problem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full-Information DDAM</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For reference, we consider first an ideal full-information setting in which all agents have direct access to the data streams of all agents.
In this case, each agent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> can evaluate the complete set of cost functions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{n,t}(\cdot)\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{n,t}(\cdot)\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the cumulative cost (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), at every time step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this idealized setting, the problem of minimizing the static or dynamic regret can be addressed via OGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, OGD yields the update <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg[\mathbf{X}_{n,t}-\eta_{n}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg[\mathbf{X}_{n,t}-\eta_{n}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the projection onto the design set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The regret properties of OGD follow directly from standard results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, OGD attains the dynamic regret</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\frac{7B^{2}}{4\eta_{n}}+\frac{\eta_{n}T\bar{G}_{n}^{2}}{2}+\frac{B}{\eta_{n}}\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>PL</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\frac{7B^{2}}{4\eta_{n}}+\frac{\eta_{n}T\bar{G}_{n}^{2}}{2}+\frac{B}{\eta_{n}}\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we have written <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{G}_{n}\triangleq\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}G_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≜</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{G}_{n}\triangleq\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}G_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
path-length is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}=\sum_{t=2}^{T}\|\mathbf{U}_{n,t-1}-\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>PL</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}=\sum_{t=2}^{T}\|\mathbf{U}_{n,t-1}-\mathbf{U}_{n,t}\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By setting an appropriate learning rate and selecting proper comparator sequences, the regret (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ III-A Full-Information DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) can be further simplified, as stated in the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By setting <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}=B\sqrt{7}/\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{2T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}=B\sqrt{7}/\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{2T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dynamic regret is upper bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}B\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{T}+\sqrt{\frac{2}{7}}\bar{G}_{n}\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\sqrt{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>PL</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}B\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{T}+\sqrt{\frac{2}{7}}\bar{G}_{n}\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\sqrt{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\left(\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(1+\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\right)\sqrt{T}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>PL</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\left(\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(1+\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}\right)\sqrt{T}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, by letting <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n,t}=\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n,t}=\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 1st item ‣ II-B Problem Definition ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the path-length vanishes, i.e., <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>PL</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PL}_{n}^{T}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the dynamic regret reduces to the static regret, yielding the upper bound</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}B\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{T}\right)=\mathcal{O}\left(N\sqrt{T}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\left(\sqrt{\frac{7}{2}}B\bar{G}_{n}\sqrt{T}\right)=\mathcal{O}\left(N\sqrt{T}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ III-A Full-Information DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates that OGD achieves a dynamic regret that scales with the path-length and thus with the degree of environmental variation.
In particular, if the environment is stationary, the dynamic regret reduces to the static regret (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ III-A Full-Information DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), which is sublinear in the time horizon <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results establish upper bounds on the regret achievable by decentralized protocols, in which each agent can only observe local data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consensus DDAM</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we consider a special case of the DDAM problem introduced in the previous section in which all the logical weights in the cost function (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) are equal, i.e., the weight matrix is set to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>𝟙𝟙</mn><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this setting, the cost functions in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) become identical for all agents <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, there exists a shared optimal AM mechanism <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all agents, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}=\mathbf{U}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{n}^{*}=\mathbf{U}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under the consensus protocol, all agents asymptotically agree on a common solution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\to\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}\to\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since this comes with no loss of generality in this setting, we focus here on <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consensus distributed OGD</span> (C-DOGD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> to address the DDAM problem in this special case.
C-DOGD is specified by an <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> doubly stochastic matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with entries <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{A}]_{n,m}=a_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{A}]_{n,m}=a_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, C-DOGD performs local gradient steps followed by consensus averaging as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\left[\sum_{m\in\mathcal{N}_{n}}a_{n,m}\mathbf{X}_{m,t}-\eta_{n}\nabla f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\left[\sum_{m\in\mathcal{N}_{n}}a_{n,m}\mathbf{X}_{m,t}-\eta_{n}\nabla f_{n,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To implement the update (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In III-B Consensus DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), each agent <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> needs to communicate with its neighbors in the set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to exchange the local AM parameters.
As a result, all edges in the graph <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are activated, each sustaining a communication load of</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\max}=2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\max}=2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">AM parameters per iteration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The static regret properties of C-DOGD follow directly from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the special case <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>𝟙𝟙</mn><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\mathbbm{1}\mathbbm{1}^{T}/N</annotation></semantics></math>, under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ II-C Technical Assumptions ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}={1}/{2\sqrt{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}={1}/{2\sqrt{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the C-DOGD protocol obtains the static regret <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, Theorem 1]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)\leq N\left(B+\frac{5-\alpha}{1-\alpha}G_{\max}^{2}\right)\sqrt{T}=\mathcal{O}\left(N\sqrt{T}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Reg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>max</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}\text{-}\mathrm{Reg}(T)\leq N\left(B+\frac{5-\alpha}{1-\alpha}G_{\max}^{2}\right)\sqrt{T}=\mathcal{O}\left(N\sqrt{T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we have defined <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\max}=\max_{n\in\mathcal{N}}G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\max}=\max_{n\in\mathcal{N}}G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectral gap of the doubly stochastic matrix <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Although C-DOGD circumvents the need for global loss information, its sublinear static regret guarantee in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Theorem 2. ‣ III-B Consensus DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) holds only in the special case where all agents aim to memorize the same information.
Furthermore, to the best of our knowledge, the dynamic regret performance of C-DOGD has yet to be investigated.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Tree-based Online Gradient Descent</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we study the DDAM problem introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> in its full generality by proposing a new protocol, <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DDAM via tree-based OGD</span> (DDAM-TOGD), which will be demonstrated to exhibit guaranteed regret properties.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DDAM-TOGD</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that in the DDAM problem introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> each agent <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> aims at memorizing data from a subset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of agents.
DDAM-TOGD addresses this problem by enabling inter-agent communication over routing trees connecting each agent <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to all relevant agents in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the physical connectivity graph <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on the delayed information received from the agents in subset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each agent <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> applies a form of OGD on the cumulative cost in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To elaborate, given the physical communication graph <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the logical weight matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, DDAM-TOGD first constructs <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> routing trees <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{T}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{T}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each tree <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rooted at agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and contains paths, i.e., sequences of contiguous edges in graph <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, from agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to all agents in subset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The construction of the trees is arbitrary, but we will introduce an optimized design based on the regret analysis in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Design of Communication Trees ‣ IV Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Tree-based Online Gradient Descent ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At each time step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sends its current iterate <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> along tree <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> toward all agents <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This information requires <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> time steps to arrive at agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of edges on the path from agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Upon receiving the query at time <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates the gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math> of the cost corresponding to the data <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{k}_{m,t},\mathbf{v}_{m,t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and sends it back along the reverse path.
We denote as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n,m}=2\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n,m}=2\tilde{\tau}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> the total communication delay incurred in the round-trip exchange between agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, the agent <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> receives the gradient <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{m,t}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t})</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+\tau_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+\tau_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the most recent gradient information available from the agents in the subset <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, at time step <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>, each agent applies the update</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg[\mathbf{X}_{n,t}-\eta_{n}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}\mathbf{G}_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}^{n}\mathbbm{1}_{\{t&gt;\tau_{n,m}\}}\Bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="1.140em" minsize="1.140em">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.140em" minsize="1.140em">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg[\mathbf{X}_{n,t}-\eta_{n}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}w_{n,m}\mathbf{G}_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}^{n}\mathbbm{1}_{\{t&gt;\tau_{n,m}\}}\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}^{n}=\nabla f_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t-\tau_{n,m}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}^{n}=\nabla f_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t-\tau_{n,m}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate.
In (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In IV-A DDAM-TOGD ‣ IV Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Tree-based Online Gradient Descent ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), the gradient corresponding to agent <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
reflecting the structure of the cumulative cost function (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Setting ‣ II Problem Statement ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
The overall DDAM-TOGD is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A DDAM-TOGD ‣ IV Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Tree-based Online Gradient Descent ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each agent <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and each edge <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n,m,e}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n,m,e}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a binary variable indicating whether the routing tree <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> includes edge <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> for delivering a message from the root node <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to a target agent <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n,m,e}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n,m,e}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if edge <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is used in the path from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n,m,e}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n,m,e}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
The overall maximum per-link capacity required by DDAM-TOGD for a given choice <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\mathcal{T}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\mathcal{T}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of trees is thus given by</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\max}^{\mathcal{T}}=2\max_{e\in\mathcal{E}}\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}C_{n,m,e}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\max}^{\mathcal{T}}=2\max_{e\in\mathcal{E}}\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}C_{n,m,e}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Note that this is generally larger than the per-link capacity (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-B Consensus DDAM ‣ III Conventional Protocols ‣ Distributed Dynamic Associative Memory via Online Convex Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) required by C-DOGD.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> DDAM-TOGD (at agent <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Learning rate sequences <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta_{n,t}\}_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta_{n,t}\}_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, logical weight <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{n,m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{n,m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and tree <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize</span> AM parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{n,1}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{n,1}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"># <span id="alg1.l3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Receive memory parameters and send losses</span></span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              Receive <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{m,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{m,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> from agent <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>  

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Send <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{n,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{n,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}}(\mathbf{X}_{m,t-\tilde{\tau}_{m,n}})</annotation></semantics></math> to agent <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>  

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"># <span id="alg1.l6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Send memory parameter and receive losses</span></span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Broadcast <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> to all agents <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math>  

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              Receive <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t-\tau_{n,m}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{m,t-\tau_{n,m}}(\mathbf{X}_{n,t-\tau_{n,m}})</annotation></semantics></math> from all agents <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{W}_{n}\backslash\{n\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"># <span id="alg1.l9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Online Gradient Descent</span></span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
